  "prompt": "Find the common rule that maps an input grid to an output grid, given the examples below. Generate a python script that takes a grid as input and transforms it according to the this rule. Anticipate that this program will be applied to several grids, not only the shown test grid.\n\nYour script must define exactly one callable entrypoint:\ndef solve(grid):\nwhere `grid` is passed as a list of lists of integers (list[list[int]]) and the function returns the transformed grid in the same format.\n\nExample 1:\n\nInput:\n0 0 8 0 8\n2 2 8 0 0\n2 2 0 0 8\n0 0 0 2 2\n8 8 0 2 2\nOutput:\n0\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:\n0 8 0 0 0 0 0\n2 2 0 8 8 8 0\n2 2 8 8 0 2 2\n0 0 8 0 0 2 2\n0 8 0 0 8 0 0\nOutput:\n8\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput:\n8 2 2 8 8 0 0\n0 2 2 0 0 0 8\n0 8 8 0 0 8 0\n0 0 8 0 0 0 8\n8 0 8 8 8 2 2\n8 0 0 0 0 2 2\nOutput:\n8\n\nExample 4:\n\nInput:\n8 8 0 0 2 2 0\n0 8 8 0 2 2 8\n0 0 0 8 0 8 0\n8 0 0 0 0 0 0\n0 2 2 0 8 0 8\n0 2 2 8 8 0 8\nOutput:\n0\n\nExample 5:\n\nInput:\n8 0 0 0 0 8 0\n0 0 2 2 0 8 0\n8 0 2 2 0 0 0\n0 0 8 0 0 8 0\n0 0 8 2 2 0 8\n8 0 0 2 2 8 0\nOutput:\n8\n\nExample 6:\n\nInput:\n8 0 0 2 2 8\n8 0 8 2 2 0\n0 0 0 0 8 0\n2 2 8 0 8 0\n2 2 0 0 0 8\n0 8 8 0 8 0\nOutput:\n0\n\nBelow is a test input grid.\n\nInput:\n2 2 8 8 0 8\n2 2 0 8 0 0\n8 8 0 0 0 8\n0 8 8 8 0 0\n8 0 8 0 0 8\n0 0 8 2 2 0\n8 0 0 2 2 0\n0 8 0 0 0 8\n\nYour final answer should just be the python script itself, no other text or markdown.",
  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    if not grid:\n        return []\n\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0])\n\n    # total occurrences of each colour\n    total = {}\n    # columnwise counts\n    col_counts = [{} for _ in range(cols)]\n\n    for r in range(rows):\n        row = grid[r]\n        for c, val in enumerate(row):\n            total[val] = total.get(val, 0) + 1\n            d = col_counts[c]\n            d[val] = d.get(val, 0) + 1\n\n    # Determine the set of colours present (we expect 0,2,8 but keep it generic)\n    colours = set(total.keys())\n\n    # number of columns where each colour appears an odd number of times\n    odd_col = {}\n    for col in col_counts:\n        for colr in colours:\n            if col.get(colr, 0) % 2 == 1:\n                odd_col[colr] = odd_col.get(colr, 0) + 1\n\n    # colours with an odd number of odd\u2011columns\n    odd_candidates = [c for c in colours if odd_col.get(c, 0) % 2 == 1]\n\n    if len(odd_candidates) == 1:\n        result = odd_candidates[0]\n    elif len(odd_candidates) > 1:\n        # choose the one with the largest total count\n        result = max(odd_candidates, key=lambda c: total.get(c, 0))\n    else:\n        # no colour has an odd number of odd\u2011columns\n        # consider colours that appear oddly in at least one column\n        have_odd = [c for c in colours if odd_col.get(c, 0) > 0]\n        if have_odd:\n            # pick the rarer of them\n            result = min(have_odd, key=lambda c: total.get(c, 0))\n        else:\n            # all zero \u2013 pick the overall rarest colour\n            result = min(colours, key=lambda c: total.get(c, 0))\n\n    return [[result]]",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    if not grid:\n        return []\n\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0])\n\n    # total occurrences of each colour\n    total = {}\n    # columnwise counts\n    col_counts = [{} for _ in range(cols)]\n\n    for r in range(rows):\n        row = grid[r]\n        for c, val in enumerate(row):\n            total[val] = total.get(val, 0) + 1\n            d = col_counts[c]\n            d[val] = d.get(val, 0) + 1\n\n    # Determine the set of colours present (we expect 0,2,8 but keep it generic)\n    colours = set(total.keys())\n\n    # number of columns where each colour appears an odd number of times\n    odd_col = {}\n    for col in col_counts:\n        for colr in colours:\n            if col.get(colr, 0) % 2 == 1:\n                odd_col[colr] = odd_col.get(colr, 0) + 1\n\n    # colours with an odd number of odd\u2011columns\n    odd_candidates = [c for c in colours if odd_col.get(c, 0) % 2 == 1]\n\n    if len(odd_candidates) == 1:\n        result = odd_candidates[0]\n    elif len(odd_candidates) > 1:\n        # choose the one with the largest total count\n        result = max(odd_candidates, key=lambda c: total.get(c, 0))\n    else:\n        # no colour has an odd number of odd\u2011columns\n        # consider colours that appear oddly in at least one column\n        have_odd = [c for c in colours if odd_col.get(c, 0) > 0]\n        if have_odd:\n            # pick the rarer of them\n            result = min(have_odd, key=lambda c: total.get(c, 0))\n        else:\n            # all zero \u2013 pick the overall rarest colour\n            result = min(colours, key=lambda c: total.get(c, 0))\n\n    return [[result]]",
